  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1229 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nA vigorous mid/upper trough over the Central Rockies was evident on \nwater vapor imagery late this morning while the mid-lvl speed max \nwas situated across the Southern High Plains. This strong mid-lvl \nflow is progged to nose into southern Kansas in the 21-00Z \ntimeframe. This is when we will see the sfc low pressure area deepen \nover parts of central and southwest KS allowing some backing of the \nsfc winds across south central Kansas allowing the dryline to \nsharpen. As the cap weakens, scattered to numerous thunderstorms are \nexpected to develop by around 21Z (possibly earlier) and spread \nrapidly north and east across the area. Moisture in the warm sector \nis abundant with late morning sfc dewpoints in the mid and upper \n60s. The environment remains favorable for supercell formation with \nfavorable 0-3km CAPE and strong low-lvl shear profiles progged as \nwe move into the mid and late afternoon hours. Any discrete or semi-\ndiscrete cells in the late afternoon and early evening hours may be \ncapable of producing a brief tornado before the activity becomes \nmore linear as we move into the evening hours. Even with a linear \nmode as we move into the evening hours, abundant 0-3KM MLCAPE/0-3km \nbulk shear would support some brief QLCS tornadoes in addition to \ndamaging winds and some more marginal hail potential. The line of \nstorms looks to shift east of southeast Kansas around midnight. \n\nScattered showers with embedded thunderstorms will remain possible \nalong the Pacific front in central and parts of south central KS as \nit moves slowly eastward through the night. We continue to see steep \nmid-lvl lapse rates as the upper low moves across the area which \nshould keep some embedded thunderstorms in the forecast for central \nKS through much of the night. The front will usher in more stable \nair as we move into the early morning hours on Tue with \nprecipitation gradually diminishing from west to east as the upper \nlow translates ENE away from the area. Highs on Tue are expected to \nremain seasonable ranging from the mid to upper 50s where showers \npersist (central and northeast KS) to the mid to upper 60s across \nfar southeast KS.\n\nShortwave ridging returns on Wed with seasonably mild and tranquil \nweather anticipated. Another shortwave trough is progged to arrive \nlate Wed night into Thu bringing increasing probabilities for \nshowers, especially across southern KS while seasonably mild \ntemperatures persist across the area.\n\nMild and dry weather will return on Friday as another mid/upper \nridge moves across the Central Plains. This will be followed by a \nclipper system digging across the Northern Plains and Upper \nMississippi Valley area late on Saturday. This system will drive a \nweak cold front south across the area Sat night into Sun but higher \nprobabilities for showers are expected to remain north and east of \nour region. The front will bring gusty winds but minimal impacts to \ntemperatures with seasonable values anticipated through the weekend.\n\nAn extended period of mild weather may arrive as we move into next \nweek as a mid/upper ridge is progged to impact the central CONUS \nbringing above average temperatures and mostly dry weather \nconditions.",
